                <pb facs="0702" n="584" sameAs="frus:frus1929v03/pg584"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">312.1722 Sandino/3: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Honduras</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Summerlin</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tegucigalpa</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-04-10T12:00:00-06:00">April 10,
                        1929—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 3:32 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>35. The Subsecretary for Foreign Affairs has just informed me by direction of the
                    President of the Republic that the Minister of Mexico has requested permission
                    for Sandino to pass unarmed through Honduras on his way to Mexico. Doctor Duron
                    appeared to view the request sympathetically, but stated that no reply has yet
                    been made to the Mexican Minister.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Managua.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Summerlin</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
